\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 When 'A.I. Layoffs' Are a Cover For Addressing Other Problems\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kalley Huang \par \uc2 1,166 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  and Block have each laid off at least 10 percent of their employees in recent months and partly blamed artificial intelligence. About 13,000 jobs were eliminated among the three companies.\par \par \uc2 But the cuts also came after big changes and growing questions about their businesses. \uc2 Meta\uc2  backed away from its big bet on the so-called metaverse, which cost the company about $80 billion. \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Brian Armstrong\uc2 , said that its business remained volatile and that there was ''a down market'' for cryptocurrency. And Block's top executive, \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2 , acknowledged that the company had grown too much during the pandemic, tripling its work force from 2019 to 2022.\par \par \uc2 Layoffs in the tech industry are accelerating, whatever the motivations of executives. So far this year, more than 150 technology companies have cut a total of at least 115,000 employees, according to Layoffs.fyi, which tracks job cuts in the industry.\par \par \uc2 That drip-drip of layoffs has become a steady stream in recent weeks. Companies slashing their staffs have run the gamut from software providers like \uc2 Atlassian\uc2  and \uc2 Autodesk\uc2 , to social networking apps like \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  and \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , to financial technology companies like \uc2 Intuit\uc2  and \uc2 PayPal\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But in more than a few cases, the recent layoffs have coincided with other business issues. Wall Street loves an A.I. story right now. That, analysts and economists say, has offered a smoke screen for companies looking to beef up profits or patch over old mistakes.\par \par \uc2 Cutting jobs to make way for A.I. is ''a nice excuse, but some of these aren't necessarily the best, most well-run companies,'' said \uc2 Mark Mahaney\uc2 , an analyst at the investment bank \uc2 Evercore\uc2 . ''They may have overhired, or they may be losing market share. There may be other issues.''\par \par \uc2 When Snap's chief executive, \uc2 Evan Spiegel\uc2 , laid off 1,000 people in April, for example, he said the company needed to turn a profit, which it has done in only three quarters since going public in 2017. But he also said A.I. was improving efficiency at the company, with ''small squads leveraging A.I. tools to drive meaningful progress across several important initiatives.''\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 's turn toward A.I. was timed with a big shift away from its giant metaverse project. During the pandemic, the company hired thousands of people to work on the effort, saying it would add 10,000 employees in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . From 2019 to 2022, \uc2 Meta\uc2  doubled in size to about 87,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 Since then, \uc2 Meta\uc2  has steadily trimmed from its augmented and virtual reality unit as it has funneled money into A.I. In April, \uc2 Meta\uc2  said it would spend $125 billion to $145 billion this year on capital expenditures like data centers, more than double its spending last year. Last month, \uc2 Meta\uc2  laid off 8,000 people, or 10 percent of its work force, even though its most recent quarterly profit was nearly $27 billion.\par \par \uc2 ''All these cuts are happening, and there are record profits,'' said Ava Sazanami, who worked for \uc2 Meta\uc2  from 2022 to 2025. A.I. ''is actually not costing any less money,'' she added. ''It is an excuse to some extent.''\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Meta\uc2  also reassigned 7,000 employees to work on A.I. tools and apps. The company has been pushing its workers to adopt A.I., factoring their use of the technology into performance reviews and tracking employees' computers to gather training data for its own A.I.\par \par \uc2 ''We're seeing more and more examples where one or two people are building something in a week that would have previously taken dozens of people months,'' \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief executive, said during a call with investors in April.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  said its layoffs, reassignments and other personnel changes varied by team. \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  and Snap declined to comment. Block did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Many other companies have said they are cutting jobs to help free up money for A.I. projects. \uc2 Intuit\uc2  laid off about 3,000 people last month so it could devote more resources to its ''big bets,'' including expanding its ''A.I.-native platform,'' \uc2 Sasan Goodarzi\uc2 , its chief executive, said in a memo to employees.\par \par \uc2 Cisco\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Chuck Robbins\uc2 , said the company would invest ''in our employees' use of A.I. across the company'' as it cut 4,000 employees last month. And \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  offered early retirement in April to roughly 7 percent of its employees in the United States, or thousands of people, as it planned to spend about $190 billion this year on capital expenditures like data centers.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the most blunt explanation for job cuts has come from \uc2 Cloudflare\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Matthew Prince\uc2 . When the company, which provides various internet services, laid off 1,100 people last month, he said in a memo to employees that the cuts were ''not a cost-cutting exercise or an assessment of individuals' performance.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Prince said his company was restructuring for ''the agentic A.I. era,'' referring to digital assistants that can do tasks by themselves. In an opinion essay in The Wall Street Journal, he said the technology would replace workers he called ''measurers'' -- people with jobs in sectors like internal audit, compliance, finance, marketing and operations -- and middle managers.\par \par \uc2 Intuit, Cisco, Microsoft and Cloudflare declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The rest of the economy has not yet seen sweeping job cuts because of A.I., said Daniel Keum, an associate professor of management at Columbia Business School.\par \par \uc2 ''There are certain segments of the labor market where we're starting to see real impact,'' like ''tech-concentrated sectors for juniors and new graduates,'' Mr. Keum said. ''If you're a junior who graduated in the past two years -- or, even worse, if you graduated this year -- then hiring is getting cut.''\par \par \uc2 But relief for tech workers doesn't appear to be on the horizon. Andy Jassy, Amazon's chief executive, said last year that the company expected to operate with fewer corporate employees in the coming years ''as we get efficiency gains from using A.I. extensively across the company.'' Amazon laid off 14,000 corporate employees in October and 16,000 more in January, saying those cuts were to reduce bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 For college graduates with computer science degrees just entering the work force, getting a job could be a struggle. In addition to the layoffs, Meta said it would close 6,000 roles it had planned to fill. Snap said it would close 300 open roles. Although A.I. companies are still hiring, the technology has led some start-ups to hire many fewer people.\par \par \uc2 ''A.I. is causing this isolated recession for college graduates now,'' Mr. Keum said. ''Is that going to slow down? My answer is no. It's going to accelerate.''\par \par \uc2 Natallie Rocha contributed reporting from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Natallie Rocha contributed reporting from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260604em640002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
