\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Casualties of A.I. Mount As Meta Lays Off 8,000\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eli Tan, Kalley Huang and Mike Isaac \par \uc2 1,088 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees have signed petitions against being tracked by A.I. and were trying to figure out who had been let go on Wednesday, as the Silicon Valley giant tries to transform into an A.I.-first company.\par \par \uc2 For the last month, employees at \uc2 Meta\uc2  have been on edge.\par \par \uc2 In April, they were told that 8,000 of them, or 10 percent of the work force, would be laid off on May 20 as \uc2 Meta\uc2  remade itself for the artificial intelligence era. On Monday, they learned that another 7,000 employees would be reassigned to new A.I. initiatives.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the ax fell. The layoffs began in Singapore, where at 4 a.m. local time emails went out to workers who were being laid off. Employees in Britain, the United States and elsewhere were notified early Wednesday morning in their respective time zones.\par \par \uc2 Many workers began exchanging somber messages remotely and checked the internal directory to figure out who on their teams was cut. On \uc2 Meta\uc2 's internal forums, hundreds reacted to the layoffs with salad emojis, their way to say ''salute.'' At least one person who was hired within the past month was laid off, two people with knowledge of the job cuts said.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 's offices were mostly empty on Wednesday after \uc2 Janelle Gale\uc2 , the company's head of human resources, told employees this week that they should work from home. On the office walls, fliers sharing a petition to stop \uc2 Meta\uc2 's new program to track employee data for A.I. training were visible, eight employees said. Some workers scavenged the offices for free snacks and laptop chargers on Monday in case they no longer had jobs by the end of the week, said the employees, who declined to be identified for fear of retaliation.\par \par \uc2 The turmoil at \uc2 Meta\uc2  -- which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  -- offers an up-close look at layoffs in the A.I. age. Job cuts at tech companies in the name of the fast-evolving technology have gathered steam. Tech workers, it is becoming clear, have been creating their own A.I. replacements. Last week, the networking giant \uc2 Cisco\uc2  said it would eliminate 4,000 jobs as it shifted more resources to A.I. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , Block and \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  also recently announced layoffs or buyouts because of the technology.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief executive, has been vocal about staking his company on A.I. He has said he wants to create ''superintelligence,'' an advanced A.I. that can act as the ultimate personal assistant. Last month, \uc2 Meta\uc2  said it would spend between $125 billion and $145 billion -- more than double what it spent in 2025 -- this year, much of it on A.I.\par \par \uc2 But the company's transformation from a social networking firm to an A.I.-first entity has been far from smooth. Employees have said the embrace of A.I. has led to anger and anxiety across \uc2 Meta\uc2 's 78,000 person work force, according to 13 current and former employees. The frustration was heightened by \uc2 Meta\uc2 's strong financial performance, with the company reporting record revenue last month, leading to questions about why job cuts were needed.\par \par \uc2 In a note to employees on Wednesday morning, Mr. Zuckerberg said he was grateful to those who were laid off for their work and optimistic about the company's A.I. efforts. More companywide layoffs were not expected this year, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''Success isn't a given. A.I. is the most consequential technology of our lifetimes,'' Mr. Zuckerberg wrote. ''The companies that lead the way will define the next generation.''\par \par \uc2 Before the cuts, hundreds of employees in New York gathered for drinks on Tuesday night to ''commiserate or celebrate, pick your poison,'' according to a copy of an invitation seen by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . The title of the event: ''Never a dull moment \u240\'00\'F0\u376\'01\'78\u171\'00\'AB\u161\'00\'A1.''\par \par \uc2 More than 1,000 employees have so far signed the petition to stop the A.I. data tracking program, while others have rallied around internal posts critiquing top leadership. But as Mr. Zuckerberg pushes forward with A.I., employees are wrestling with how, or whether, they can do anything to change course.\par \par \uc2 ''A.I. is a freight train, but the future is not a foregone conclusion. It's not too late to pump the brakes and consider how we, society, want to go about this,'' Mack Ward, a software engineer at \uc2 Meta\uc2 , wrote in a post to employees this month, which was liked by over 2,000 people, encouraging them to sign the petition. ''Speaking up is never easy, but 'easy' isn't what you were hired to do.''\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bosworth\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief technology officer, addressed some concerns in a question-and-answer session last week. There are ''a tremendous number of employees feeling anxieties about their futures,'' he said, according to a recording of the meeting reviewed by The Times. ''It's all bad. I'm not going to try to sugarcoat that.''\par \par \uc2 Executives have taken steps to retain workers they see as key talent. One director-level employee was offered additional equity worth roughly $500,000 to stay, two people familiar with the matter said, a tactic that \uc2 Meta\uc2  also used to keep certain employees during its last round of layoffs. Some refused the offers and left, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Workers who were laid off were given 16 weeks of severance pay, plus an additional two weeks for every year they worked at the company.\par \par \uc2 This month, Meta began enlisting hundreds of employees to work for a new team led by Maher Saba, a vice president of engineering, called Applied A.I. and Engineering, five of the employees said. Some workers began referring to the effort as a ''Draft.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Saba's new team, which so far has around 2,000 employees, will use the data gathered by the employee tracking program to create A.I. tools, four employees said. It will have fewer layers of management than other parts of Meta, with around 50 workers reporting to each manager. Those who joined the group would be safe from the layoffs, the company said.\par \par \uc2 In an email to managers this month about the new A.I. team, Meta instructed them to emphasize to employees that it was ''a high priority initiative, directly from Mark.''\par \par \uc2 Participation was not optional, the message said.\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260521em5l0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
