\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 L.I.R.R. Strike Causes a Rush Hour by Hour\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefanos Chen and Ashley Southall \par \uc2 1,501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Commuters took lengthy detours on the third day of a strike by \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  workers, who had gone without raises for years. Workers and transit officials reached a deal late Monday.\par \par \uc2 Transit authorities and workers' unions reached a deal on Monday to end a strike that shut down the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , America's busiest passenger rail service, and upended the routines of a quarter million weekday riders.\par \par \uc2 Officials said that service on the railroad -- which carries about 270,000 people a day between New York City and its eastern suburbs -- will gradually resume on Tuesday, with full service restored in time for the evening rush hour.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  sounded triumphant at a news conference alongside officials from the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2  late Monday, emphasizing that the deal would avoid raising taxes and fares. Officials did not disclose details of the labor agreement, noting that the contract must still be ratified.\par \par \uc2 ''I always believed we could reach a good, fair compromise that achieved two principles,'' Ms. Hochul said. ''Protecting affordability for Long Islanders and commuters, while giving fair wages to employees.''The deal signaled the end of a nightmare for many commuters who rely on the railroad. Thousands of them got a glimpse of what a protracted labor dispute could look like as they took a patchwork of buses, car pools and subway trains to clock in on time. At least one company rented hotel rooms to help employees avoid the headache, while some workers heeded calls to work from home.\par \par \uc2 ''Three hours going to work is just not worth it -- my job doesn't pay that much,'' Vanessa Zhang, 38, said in an interview Monday morning at the Hicksville station on Long Island, where transit officials were providing a limited number of shuttle buses to Queens.\par \par \uc2 She had missed the last bus to get to her retail sales job in Midtown Manhattan by a hair. Her only other options were buses on a different, unfamiliar network, or a pricey \uc2 Uber\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I'll take a sick day,'' she said, before heading home.\par \par \uc2 Members of the five unions that represent about half the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  work force -- more than 3,500 employees, including engineers, signal workers and others who are vital to train operations -- walked off the job early Saturday morning, after three years of failed efforts to secure higher wages. They have not received a raise since 2022.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Sexton, the national vice president of the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , said the unions wanted to discuss the specifics with their members before telling the public. Still, he said he expected the workers covered by the deal to ratify it.\par \par \uc2 While some details of the agreement were still unclear, the deal included what effectively amounted to a 4.5 percent wage increase in 2026, over a period slightly longer than a year, according to three people familiar with the negotiations. The unions previously sought a 5 percent increase, while the M.T.A.'s offer was closer to 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown was becoming a painful ordeal for riders, a major economic strain on the region and a growing political liability for Ms. Hochul, who is seeking re-election this year.\par \par \uc2 The state comptroller's office had said before the weekend that the strike could cost the region $61 million a day in lost economic activity.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Monday, Gary Dellaverson, a former M.T.A. executive and labor negotiator who advised the authority in the talks, said he ''had no confidence'' that a deal was imminent.\par \par \uc2 But urgency was building.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York Knicks\uc2  will face the \uc2 Cleveland Cavaliers\uc2  at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  on Tuesday night in the first game of the Eastern Conference finals, and thousands of fans were expecting to take the L.I.R.R. to the series. Memorial Day, when thousands of New York City residents flock to Long Island, is less than a week away.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in negotiations, the unions said they were willing to accept a retroactive 9.5 percent wage increase covering the last three years -- the same deal the M.T.A. offered several other transit and civil service unions in recent months.\par \par \uc2 But they were also seeking an up to 5 percent raise in the current year, a demand that exceeds what the agency has offered to other unions.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. has argued that the unions' wage demands could lead to higher fares for passengers or reductions in service. The authority negotiates with more than 80 different unions, and acceding to much higher wage increases with one group could force it to pay substantially more when other contracts come due.\par \par \uc2 The unions pointed to the recommendations of two federal review panels that supported a higher wage increase than the M.T.A. had offered, and said they were unwilling to cede other parts of their contract, like changes to longstanding work rules, that could reduce their overall income.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the two sides remained about 1 percentage point apart on wage increases for 2026. The unions rejected an M.T.A. proposal that would have required new employees to cover more health care costs under a model different from the one current employees use.\par \par \uc2 Cash compensation for members of the five holdout unions averaged over $136,000 in 2025, according to M.T.A. figures, making them among the highest-paid rail workers in the nation.\par \par \uc2 But leaders of the negotiating unions argued that their workers don't make enough money to keep up with the cost of living in one of the country's most expensive metro areas.\par \par \uc2 In response to the strike, which shut down 126 rail stations across the region, the M.T.A. began providing free shuttle bus service between a handful of stations on Long Island and two subway stops in Queens.\par \par \uc2 The authority warned that the buses could accommodate only about 13,000 riders during the morning and evening rushes, but by Monday afternoon, fewer than 2,200 riders had used the service, according to the M.T.A. Reserving the buses costs the agency about $550,000 a day.\par \par \uc2 Many stranded riders said they sympathized with the unions -- but interviews suggested they had their limits.\par \par \uc2 Mandy Ramzan, 50, who lives in Long Island City in Queens, joined a long line of rerouted commuters at a bus stop on Roosevelt Avenue in Flushing.\par \par \uc2 She was transferring from the No. 7 train in the hopes of getting to her job in Great Neck in Nassau County, where she is the director of an assisted living center.\par \par \uc2 That detour was adding an hour to her morning commute, she said.\par \par \uc2 Standing in line with a warming iced coffee, Ms. Ramzan said she had mixed feelings about the labor dispute.\par \par \uc2 ''Listen, people deserve to be paid. I get it,'' she said. ''But there are also a lot of commuters who are really feeling it this morning.''\par \par \uc2 The strike was unwelcome for Ms. Hochul, whose opponent in the November governor's race, Bruce Blakeman, a Republican and the Nassau County executive, has close ties to Long Island. Ms. Hochul lost the region in the 2022 election.\par \par \uc2 ''This strike never should have happened,'' Mr. Blakeman said in a statement. ''Union leaders do not trust Kathy Hochul when she says she doesn't have the money for their workers,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul has directed some of her frustration at President Trump, whom she has blamed for increasing the odds of a strike. The president controls the National Mediation Board, which last year released the unions from mediation, a decision that cleared the path for a possible walkout.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, Mr. Trump said the governor ''knows, full well, that I have NOTHING TO DO WITH IT,'' before expressing his support for Mr. Blakeman.\par \par \uc2 The suspension was expected to have an outsize effect on middle-class workers who cannot work from home, both on Long Island and in New York City.\par \par \uc2 A growing number of Queens residents rely on the Long Island Rail Road, and an influx of passengers from the suburbs boarding the subway in that borough could add to the challenge.\par \par \uc2 Queens has some busy L.I.R.R. stations, including the hub at Jamaica, where many train lines converge and where riders can connect to the subway and the AirTrain to Kennedy International Airport.\par \par \uc2 The last time Long Island Rail Road workers walked out, in 1994, the dispute was resolved in two days. The longest strike on the railroad occurred in the summer of 1960, when service was suspended for 26 days as workers pushed for better wages and other demands. It led to the institution of a five-day workweek for the rail workers.\par \par \uc2 Sean Piccoli, Nate Schweber and Grace Ashford contributed reporting. Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Sean Piccoli, Nate Schweber and Grace Ashford contributed reporting. Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260519em5j00078\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
