\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Volley of Talks Fails to Prevent L.I.R.R. Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefanos Chen, Ashley Southall and Ellen Yan \par \uc2 1,604 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This is the first strike on the service in more than 30 years. It comes after three years of failed contract negotiations, two federal interventions and a volley of last-minute bargaining.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers for the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  walked off the job early Saturday morning, staging the first strike in more than 30 years for America's busiest passenger railway and grinding service to a halt.\par \par \uc2 After three years of failed contract negotiations, two federal interventions and a volley of last-minute bargaining, unions representing about half of the work force decided to take to the picket line to protest what they called insufficient wage increases.\par \par \uc2 Five unions representing more than 3,500 workers -- including engineers, signalmen and machinists -- called the strike after contract discussions with the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , the state agency that runs the railroad, fell apart.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Sexton, a vice president of the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , one of the unions, said the two sides could not agree on raises in 2026, or on issues like health care contributions.\par \par \uc2 ''We are truly sorry that we're in this situation,'' Mr. Sexton said at a midnight news conference outside of M.T.A. headquarters. ''But this is why you have to take collective bargaining seriously.''\par \par \uc2 More than 270,000 daily riders rely on the service to travel between New York City and Long Island, a sprawl of suburbs and bedroom communities where many of the region's workers live.\par \par \uc2 On the street level at Jamaica station in Queens, several yards from about 100 picketing workers, Brandon MacDowell, a \uc2 Baruch College\uc2  student, was adjusting to a new commute. He said he would usually take the L.I.R.R. to Penn Station, but for final exams on Saturday, his mother had to drive him to the station so he could take the subway.\par \par \uc2 Mr. MacDowell, 22, said he would repeat the journey on Sunday for the last exam of his school year and considered himself lucky. ''I have friends that work in the city and they're freaking out,'' Mr. MacDowell said.\par \par \uc2 The strike comes as Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat, seeks re-election later this year. The governor, who lost Long Island in the previous election, is being challenged by the Nassau County executive, Bruce Blakeman, a Republican with close ties to the region.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul said in a statement that while her administration has made investments in the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , the unions' demands could force her to increase taxes or raise fares by as much as 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 ''The L.I.R.R. is more stable now than it has been for generations,'' she said. ''The decision by some unions to strike over demands that would threaten that progress is reckless.''\par \par \uc2 Janno Lieber\uc2 , the chief executive of the M.T.A., said the authority was willing to increase its offer for higher wages but the unions were unwilling to compromise. He said the M.T.A. could not make a deal that ''implodes'' its budget.\par \par \uc2 To mitigate the shutdown, the M.T.A. said it would provide free shuttle buses between six locations on Long Island and two subway stations in Queens.\par \par \uc2 But the service will be unable to accommodate all the riders who rely on the railroad, and it won't begin until Monday, leaving many scrambling for weekend travel alternatives.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the \uc2 New York Mets\uc2  faced the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  at Citi Field in Queens, where thousands of Long Island-based fans would usually arrive by rail.\par \par \uc2 But before the game on Saturday, the normally bustling entrance to the Mets-Willets Point Long Island Rail Road station near Citi Field in Queens was blocked off by metal barricades.\par \par \uc2 Irvin Hernandez, 21, and Sofia Lopez, 20, vendors who sell water and Gatorade outside the stadium, stood on the overpass that connects the station to the ballpark.\par \par \uc2 On most game days, they would have already seen thousands of fans and sold dozens of drinks. But by 6 p.m. on Saturday, Mr. Hernandez and Ms. Lopez had yet to make a single sale.\par \par \uc2 ''There's nothing else we can do,'' Mr. Hernandez said.\par \par \uc2 For many fans from Long Island, the only way to get to the game was to drive.\par \par \uc2 Ken Nolan, 66, a \uc2 Yankees\uc2  fan from Bellmore, said he and his daughter left three hours before the first pitch to make sure they would get there on time -- an hour earlier than they normally would have.\par \par \uc2 Still, he said, ''It was a real pain. The roads were crazy.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Nolan -- who wore a T-shirt with a large image of Mickey Mantle, the Bronx slugger -- said he was already dreading the commute home.\par \par \uc2 ''If the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  are up by a lot, we might be leaving in the 7th inning,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 If the Mets are winning, he said, ''We weep, and then we cry, and then we get in the car and go home to beat the traffic.''\par \par \uc2 On Saturday morning at the entrance of the Morris Park maintenance facility in Queens, about 20 picketing workers held up signs with messages, including, ''Wages, Not Waste'' and ''No Contract, No Work.''\par \par \uc2 Several strikers had been there since midnight and said the picketing locations would be staffed by members around the clock.\par \par \uc2 Phillip Diaz, an electrician from Fresh Meadows who has worked for the Long Island Rail Road for eight years, said he worried about being able to afford his daughter's tuition at the University of Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 ''We feel bad for the public,'' Mr. Diaz, 42, said. ''Most of these people are neighbors, friends, and family. We don't need this any more than they do.''\par \par \uc2 The Long Island Rail Road carried 82 million customers last year. Most were weekday commuters on their way to jobs in New York City.\par \par \uc2 This is the first strike on the railroad since 1994, when a two-day suspension shut down the service.\par \par \uc2 The state comptroller's office said on Friday that the strike could cost the region $61 million a day in lost economic activity.\par \par \uc2 Discussions between the unions and management broke down on the final day of negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The unions were seeking a retroactive 9.5 percent wage increase covering the last three years -- the same deal the M.T.A. offered several other transit and civil service unions in recent months. But they were also seeking a 5 percent raise in the current year, a demand that exceeded what the M.T.A. has offered to other unions.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. countered with a 3 percent raise for 2026, plus a lump-sum cash payment, which it said would avoid upending negotiations with more than 80 other unions.\par \par \uc2 By Friday afternoon, the two sides were about 1 percentage point apart on wage increases, but were unwilling to compromise further.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the negotiating unions have argued that their workers don't make enough money to keep up with the cost of living in one of the country's most expensive metro areas. They have not received raises since 2022.\par \par \uc2 Cash compensation for members of the five holdout unions averaged over $136,000 in 2025, according to M.T.A. figures, making them among the highest-paid rail workers in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in negotiations, the M.T.A. had also sought to eliminate a number of work rules that often require higher pay for certain tasks. The unions declined to do so.\par \par \uc2 For instance, if an engineer drives a diesel train at the start of a shift but is asked to switch to an electric train in the same day, the M.T.A. must compensate that worker with two days' pay. If, on the same day, the engineer is asked to switch from driving passengers to driving a train back to a yard for maintenance or storage, that worker is entitled to a third day of pay.\par \par \uc2 These penalty payments added almost 15 percent to the average engineer's compensation in 2024, the M.T.A. said.\par \par \uc2 The Long Island Rail Road has an annual operating budget of $2.2 billion, and labor accounts for nearly three-fourths of that budget.\par \par \uc2 Unlike much of the M.T.A. work force, which is prevented from striking because it is governed by different rules, Long Island Rail Road workers are covered by a 1926 federal law called the Railway Labor Act.\par \par \uc2 The law was designed to prevent major service disruptions by requiring mediation and an extended review period before a strike is authorized.\par \par \uc2 But in an unusual move, the federal agency that oversees such disputes, the National Mediation Board, last year released the unions from mediation, a decision that cleared the path for a possible walkout.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul said on Saturday that she blamed the Trump administration for cutting the mediation short, and increasing the likelihood of a strike.\par \par \uc2 A strike was postponed twice within the past year, after the unions requested the intervention of two federally appointed review panels. The three-person panels, which were appointed by President Trump, said the unions should be paid more than what the M.T.A. was offering, but their suggestions are not binding.\par \par \uc2 The unions on Saturday declined to say how long the strike might last.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't know when it will end,'' Gilman Lang, the general chairman of the Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen, said in a statement. ''It shouldn't have begun.''\par \par \uc2 Miles G. Cohen contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Miles G. Cohen contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260517em5h0005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
