\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  Strike Shuts Down Busiest U.S. Passenger Rail Service\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefanos Chen \par \uc2 1,375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This is the first strike on the service in more than 30 years. It comes after three years of failed contract negotiations, two federal interventions and a volley of last-minute bargaining.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers for the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  walked off the job early Saturday morning, staging the first strike in more than 30 years for America's busiest passenger railway and grinding service to a halt.\par \par \uc2 After three years of failed contract negotiations, two federal interventions and a volley of last-minute bargaining, unions representing about half of the work force decided to take to the picket line to protest what they called insufficient wage increases.\par \par \uc2 Five unions representing more than 3,500 workers -- including engineers, signalmen and machinists -- called the strike after contract discussions with the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , the state agency that runs the railroad, fell apart.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Sexton, a vice president of the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , one of the unions, said the two sides could not agree on raises in 2026, or on issues like health care contributions.\par \par \uc2 ''We are truly sorry that we're in the this situation,'' Mr. Sexton said at a midnight news conference outside of M.T.A. headquarters. ''But this is why you have to take collective bargaining seriously.''\par \par \uc2 The strike sets up a cascade of travel woes for the more than 270,000 daily riders who rely on the service to travel between New York City and Long Island, a sprawl of suburbs and bedroom communities where many of the region's workers live.\par \par \uc2 It also comes as Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat, seeks re-election later this year. The governor, who lost Long Island in the previous election, is being challenged by the Nassau County executive, Bruce Blakeman, a Republican with close ties to the region.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul said in a statement that while her administration has made investments in the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , the unions' demands could force her to increase taxes or raise fares by as much as 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 ''The L.I.R.R. is more stable now than it has been for generations. The decision by some unions to strike over demands that would threaten that progress is reckless,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Janno Lieber\uc2 , the chief executive of the M.T.A., said the authority was willing to increase its offer for higher wages but the unions were unwilling to compromise. He said the M.T.A. could not make a deal that ''implodes'' its budget.\par \par \uc2 To mitigate the shutdown, the M.T.A. said it would provide free shuttle buses between six locations on Long Island and two subway stations in Queens.\par \par \uc2 But the service will be unable to accommodate all the riders who rely on the railroad, and it won't begin until Monday, leaving many scrambling for weekend travel alternatives.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the \uc2 New York Mets\uc2  are set to face the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  at Citi Field in Queens, where thousands of Long Island-based fans were expected to arrive by rail.\par \par \uc2 If the strike does not end by Monday morning, buses will shuttle riders between the Bay Shore, Hicksville and Mineola L.I.R.R. stations, as well as Hempstead Lake State Park near the Lakeview station, and the A train stop at Howard Beach-JFK Airport. And buses from the Huntington and Ronkonkoma stations will take riders to and from the F train stop at Jamaica-179 Street.\par \par \uc2 The buses to Queens are expected to run every 10 minutes from 4:30 a.m. to 9 a.m., and afternoon shuttles back to Long Island could run from 3 p.m. to 7 p.m. They will be able to handle up to 13,000 riders during the morning rush and another 13,000 in the evening.\par \par \uc2 There will be a limited number of buses running in the non-peak direction at some of the stations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  carried 82 million customers last year. Most were weekday commuters on their way to jobs in New York City, but an increasing number of passengers were using the service on weekends.\par \par \uc2 This is the first strike on the railroad since 1994, when a two-day suspension shut down the service.\par \par \uc2 Long Island residents have become even more dependent on jobs in New York City since the coronavirus pandemic, said Tom Wright, the president of the Regional Plan Association, an urban planning think tank.\par \par \uc2 More than 300,000 Long Island residents work in New York City, according to a 2022 report by the group.\par \par \uc2 The state comptroller's office said on Friday that the strike could cost the region $61 million a day in lost economic activity.\par \par \uc2 Discussions between the unions and management broke down on the final day of negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The unions were seeking a retroactive 9.5 percent wage increase covering the last three years -- the same deal the M.T.A. offered several other transit and civil service unions in recent months. But they were also seeking a 5 percent raise in the current year, a demand that exceeded what the M.T.A. has offered to other unions.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. countered with a 3 percent raise for 2026, plus a lump-sum cash payment, which it said would avoid upending negotiations with more than 80 other unions.\par \par \uc2 By Friday afternoon, the two sides were about 1 percentage point apart on wage increases, but were unwilling to compromise further.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the negotiating unions have argued that their workers don't make enough money to keep up with the cost of living in one of the country's most expensive metro areas. They have not received raises since 2022.\par \par \uc2 Cash compensation for members of the five holdout unions averaged over $136,000 in 2025, according to M.T.A. figures, making them among the highest-paid rail workers in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in negotiations, the M.T.A. had also sought to eliminate a number of work rules that often require higher pay for certain tasks. The unions declined to do so.\par \par \uc2 For instance, if an engineer drives a diesel train at the start of a shift but is asked to switch to an electric train in the same day, the M.T.A. must compensate that worker with two days' pay. If, on the same day, the engineer is asked to switch from driving passengers to driving a train back to a yard for maintenance or storage, that worker is entitled to a third day of pay.\par \par \uc2 These penalty payments added almost 15 percent to the average engineer's compensation in 2024, the M.T.A. said.\par \par \uc2 The Long Island Rail Road has an annual operating budget of $2.2 billion, and labor accounts for nearly three-fourths of that budget.\par \par \uc2 Unlike much of the M.T.A. work force, which is prevented from striking because it is governed by different rules, Long Island Rail Road workers are covered by a 1926 federal law called the Railway Labor Act.\par \par \uc2 The law was designed to prevent major service disruptions by requiring mediation and an extended review period before a strike is authorized.\par \par \uc2 But in an unusual move, the federal agency that oversees such disputes, the National Mediation Board, last year released the unions from mediation, a decision that cleared the path for a possible walkout.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul said on Saturday that she blamed the Trump administration for cutting the mediation short, and increasing the likelihood of a strike.\par \par \uc2 A strike was postponed twice within the past year, after the unions requested the intervention of two federally appointed review panels. The three-person panels, which were appointed by President Trump, said the unions should be paid more than what the M.T.A. was offering, but their suggestions are not binding.\par \par \uc2 The unions on Saturday declined to say how long the strike might last. Workers were preparing to picket at a number of stations over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 ''This is an open-ended strike,'' Gilman Lang, the general chairman of the Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't know when it will end. It shouldn't have begun.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260516em5g0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
