\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to Cut 300 Jobs At Its Corporate Offices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Creswell \par \uc2 371 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The company said it would close four regional offices and take a $400 million charge related to the changes.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  will eliminate 300 corporate jobs in the United States and close four regional offices as part of its turnaround effort, the company said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 It said it would take about $400 million in charges for expenses related to the office closures and severance pay to employees. None of the layoffs or closures affect its stores.\par \par \uc2 In an emailed statement, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesman said the actions were part of the Seattle-based company's turnaround strategy and an effort to ''return the company to durable, profitable growth.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  eliminated nearly 2,000 corporate jobs and closed hundreds of underperforming stores.\par \par \uc2 The company said that it would review its international corporate organization and that additional cuts at those offices were likely, but it did not specify the timing or how many employees could be affected.\par \par \uc2 Regional offices will close in Atlanta, Chicago, Dallas and Burbank, Calif., \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said. Along with its Seattle headquarters, it will maintain offices in New York, Toronto and Coral Gables, Fla., as well as in Nashville, where it is expanding.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs and office closures are part of an effort by \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , the chief executive, to put \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  on better financial footing. Since taking over in 2024, he has tackled many longstanding customer complaints, from long waits for coffees to a lack of seating in stores.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that the improvements are bringing customers back. Sales at U.S. stores open for a year or more jumped 7.1 percent for the quarter that ended March 29, compared with a year earlier. Still, some Wall Street analysts remain wary of the turnaround, noting that last year's sales were particularly weak, so the comparison was easier, and that some customer traffic moved from stores that were shuttered last fall.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, Mr. Niccol is spending a lot of money to update technology, hire more baristas to make coffee and renovate stores to make them cozier. That, along with higher coffee costs from tariffs, has squeezed operating profits for the company.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260516em5g00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
