\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  to Cut 700 Jobs As A.I. Reshapes Industry\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natallie Rocha \par \uc2 516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The largest U.S. crypto exchange said it was cutting jobs because of cryptocurrency market volatility and to ''optimize'' for the artificial intelligence era.\par \par \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 , the largest U.S. cryptocurrency exchange, said on Tuesday that it planned to lay off about 14 percent of its workers amid volatility in the crypto market and as artificial intelligence changes how people work.\par \par \uc2 The cuts affect about 700 of the company's nearly 5,000 employees, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  said in a corporate filing, and the restructuring will cost about $50 million to $60 million in severance payments, termination benefits and other expenses.\par \par \uc2 ''A.I. is bringing a profound shift in how companies operate, and we're reshaping \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  to lead in this new era,'' \uc2 Brian Armstrong\uc2 , \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 's chief executive, wrote in a social media post about the layoffs. ''This is a new way of working, and we need to leverage A.I. across every facet of our jobs.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Armstrong said \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  planned to have smaller teams, with people who manage A.I. agents -- digital bots that can handle coding tasks -- as well as human managers who would need to get ''their hands dirty alongside their teams.''\par \par \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  is the latest tech company to point to A.I. as the reason for cutting jobs. While many industries have debated how the powerful technology might change the way they work, the one industry that is unquestionably being disrupted is the tech industry itself. That's partly because A.I. tools have become stronger at generating code, which affects the software engineers who underpin many digital businesses.\par \par \uc2 In February, Block, the financial technology company, laid of 40 percent of its workers, or roughly 4,000 people, citing A.I.'s rapid improvement. Last month, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 , said it planned to cut 10 percent of its work force, or roughly 8,000 employees, and close another 6,000 open roles, as it spends heavily on developing A.I. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  also offered early retirement to thousands of long-serving employees last month as it looks to thin its ranks amid major A.I. investments.\par \par \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 , founded in San Francisco in 2012, is a cryptocurrency exchange that allows people to buy and sell digital currencies like Bitcoin. The company has said its revenue depends upon the price of crypto assets and the volume of trades on its platform.\par \par \uc2 In his post, Mr. Armstrong said A.I. had led to ''an inflection point.'' He said the biggest risk was not to take any action and so his company was ''adjusting early and deliberately to rebuild \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  to be lean, fast, and A.I.-native.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Armstrong also cited cryptocurrency market volatility as a reason to restructure now, saying, ''We're currently in a down market and need to adjust our cost structure now.''\par \par \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  would ultimately have fewer layers under the chief executive and chief operating officer, he said, and would move more quickly and efficiently.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260506em560008h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
