\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Graduates Forced to Reset Job Ambitions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sydney Ember \par \uc2 1,155 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Young people aiming to build careers are entering fields they had not considered to find their footing.\par \par \uc2 Sadie Parker was fretting about finding a job.\par \par \uc2 Many people she knew were having trouble landing work. She had wanted to join the Foreign Service, but she was worried that federal spending cuts could limit her options. She was petrified that artificial intelligence would wipe out other entry-level jobs.\par \par \uc2 ''I was extremely anxious,'' said Ms. Parker, 22, who will graduate in June from the \uc2 University of California, Santa Barbara\uc2 , with a double major in political science and economics.\par \par \uc2 To improve her chances, she broadened her search, applying for positions in consulting and other fields. She spent hours on a cover letter for a job at \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , the accounting firm, advising the California state government.\par \par \uc2 ''I was like, wow, this looks so interesting,'' she said. ''The next day, I got a rejection.''\par \par \uc2 Recent college graduates are facing the most dismal and unpredictable job market in years. Employers overall are hiring fewer workers, dimming the prospects in particular for first-time entrants to the labor market. The rise of A.I. and its abilities are intensifying fears that entry-level jobs will disappear forever.\par \par \uc2 Junior-level postings on the job site Indeed fell 7 percent in 2025 from the previous year, according to a report the company released last week.\par \par \uc2 ''As a job seeker, you're having to work a lot harder to land that same job now because the competition has just really stiffened in the last couple years,'' said Cory Stahle, an economist at Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Those forces have transformed the spring graduation season into a bruising ordeal for many of America's youngest degree holders.\par \par \uc2 In interviews and in responses to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  survey, some college seniors and recent graduates said they had applied to more than 100 jobs without securing so much as a first-round interview. A number have resorted to tracking their applications using detailed Excel spreadsheets.\par \par \uc2 There is a swelling collective suspicion that A.I. is rejecting applications before human recruiters ever lay eyes on them.\par \par \uc2 The hunt has frustrated nascent career dreams and forced many job seekers to recalibrate their postgraduation plans. Some are working as servers at pizza joints, as baristas at coffee shops and in other jobs that do not require college degrees. Others have plans to attend graduate school to avoid the labor market altogether. Whereas embarking on a career used to be a goal after college, increasingly it is having any job at all.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm, like, is it me or is it really, like, the market right now?'' said Natalia Martinez, 24, a senior at the \uc2 University of Central Florida\uc2 . ''I just feel like it's so hard for somebody to take a chance on a college graduate.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Martinez said she had applied to 150 jobs since February -- ''really anything that comes up in my area,'' she said, including for positions as a receptionist or medical assistant -- but she has not been successful. She spends sleepless nights doom-scrolling for jobs on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  and Indeed and is preparing for the possibility that she may have to move back in with her parents after graduation.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel like I'm doing everything that I possibly can,'' she said. ''I just want some kind of path.''\par \par \uc2 Angst on college campuses about future employment is perennial and escalates during hiring slowdowns, when companies are more reluctant to bring on inexperienced workers. A front-page article in The Times in April 1991, for instance, took note of ''traumatized seniors'' whose job searches had ''become a disheartening struggle of r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9s, rejection and uncertainty.''\par \par \uc2 But fueled by the frenzy around A.I. and public prognostications of job market ruin, the typical jitters have hardened into dread.\par \par \uc2 ''Students are definitely nervous,'' said Sean McGowan, the director of employer relations at \uc2 Carnegie Mellon University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 College graduates typically do better during economic downturns than workers without degrees. And while there have been dire predictions about A.I.'s effect on employment, the technology so far has not led to widespread job losses.\par \par \uc2 Still, the challenging job market has revived age-old questions among college students and recent graduates about whether going to college is worth it.\par \par \uc2 ''I thought getting a college degree was the answer to everything,'' said Lucy Kinyanjui, 22, a senior at \uc2 North Carolina Agricultural and Technical State University\uc2 . ''I feel like we just have to wait it out. I feel like getting my degree now is kind of useless.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kinyanjui, who juggles classes with work as a server at Topgolf, said she was constantly thinking about applying for more lucrative jobs that aligned with a career in health care. But she is concerned that her degree, in liberal studies, will not appeal to potential employers, especially in such a tough job market. She is thinking about getting a master's degree eventually in the hope that it will make her more employable.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm afraid of the rejection that I'm going to face,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Johnathon McCartney, 23, has felt similarly discouraged. A senior at the University of Florida who transferred from Colby College in Maine after his freshman year, Mr. McCartney studied public relations and wanted to get a job in communications. He focused his search on the Louisville, Ky., area so he could live near his girlfriend, but snubs piled up, including from a local P.R. firm.\par \par \uc2 ''I applied for an internship with them and I interviewed, and I didn't even get that,'' he said. ''Who is this state-level firm taking for a P.R. internship if not me?''\par \par \uc2 Mr. McCartney recently accepted a remote job as an immigration services officer with the federal government.\par \par \uc2 ''I just feel fortunate or grateful that this is an opportunity that ended up working out for me,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Parker, the senior at U.C. Santa Barbara, the sense of urgency was growing. None of her applications seemed to be gaining traction with employers. As A.I.'s technology improved, she wondered if she was running out of time to find an entry-level job.\par \par \uc2 ''I was very much like, OK, I got to make sure I do this now,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 When one of her friends suggested that she apply for a job as a finance associate at a large health technology company, she jumped at the chance even though she did not have experience in finance. She reached out to the company's recruiters on LinkedIn and set up an informational chat.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of three months, Ms. Parker had one interview with the company, then another, and another.\par \par \uc2 ''It was kind of a stressful process,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Three weeks after her third interview, in mid-December, she heard back for the last time.\par \par \uc2 She had gotten the job.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260504em540004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
