\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Criticizes Prediction Markets Family Invests In\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Protess \par \uc2 1,119 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The White House has warned staff not to wager on government decisions, but his family's involvement with these firms undermines the president's message.\par \par \uc2 When a U.S. soldier was indicted on Thursday on charges of using classified information to place prediction market bets, it seemed to confirm President Trump's lament just hours before that ''the whole world unfortunately has become somewhat of a casino.''\par \par \uc2 ''I was never much in favor of it,'' Mr. Trump said from the Oval Office, when asked about concerns that federal employees might be leveraging insider information on the prediction markets. ''I don't like it conceptually. It is what it is. I'm not happy with any of that stuff.''\par \par \uc2 Yet Mr. Trump and his family stand to profit from the very same industry.\par \par \uc2 The president's publicly traded media company unveiled its own prediction market product last year. And the president's eldest son, \uc2 Donald Trump Jr.\uc2 , has ties to two of the industry's top firms, including \uc2 Polymarket\uc2 , the platform that prosecutors say was used by the soldier for well-timed bets.\par \par \uc2 The result, ethics experts say, is a jarring juxtaposition between Mr. Trump's public comments and his family's private business.\par \par \uc2 While the president's criticism of the prediction markets raised the prospect of new regulation, the reality is that little is likely to change. Last year, Mr. Trump's administration backed away from enforcement efforts against \uc2 Polymarket\uc2 , and it is unclear whether regulators will adopt any new oversight measures.\par \par \uc2 ''Presidential statements used to be the gold standard, but this is not true for President Trump,'' Jeffrey A. Engel, founding director of the Center for Presidential History at \uc2 Southern Methodist University\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 ''What he does is more important than what he says,'' Mr. Engel said, adding, ''I don't think his comments will bother the prediction markets in the least.\par \par \uc2 Prediction markets, a fast-growing industry, allow consumers to place wagers on the likelihood of a future event, from cricket match scores to the release date of an album by the Canadian rap star Drake. But bettors can also weigh in on military and national security matters around the world. Some of those outcomes could be known or affected by a small group of decision makers, including those in Mr. Trump's inner circle.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump made his remarks on Thursday, just hours before federal prosecutors in Manhattan charged Master Sgt. Gannon Ken Van Dyke, an Army Special Forces soldier who had helped capture Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro of Venezuela. Sgt. Van Dyke, prosecutors said, made more than $400,000 by betting on different outcomes related to Venezuela after learning of the operation.\par \par \uc2 He is not the only government employee suspected of placing well-timed bets on Venezuela and the war in Iran. There was a surge of suspicious trading related to the war last month. In response, Congress is considering legislation to limit government officials' use of prediction markets, while officials in some states are also considering new regulations.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  of Michigan, the lead Democratic sponsor of legislation that would bar any public official from placing prediction-market bets using nonpublic information they learn in the course of their jobs, has warned about ''operational risk'' and threats to national security.\par \par \uc2 It remains unclear whether the Republican-led Congress will advance the bipartisan bill. Asked about the president's son's ties to \uc2 Polymarket\uc2  and \uc2 Kalshi\uc2 , Ms. Slotkin said in an interview last month that she feared the White House could throw up barriers to passing the bill by pressuring Republicans not to support it.\par \par \uc2 ''It's too politically dicey,'' she said, adding: ''There is not a single important issue of the day where I don't feel the shadow of Trump and his sons.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Davis Ingle, a spokesman for the White House, said: ''President Trump has been crystal clear: While he seeks a strong and profitable stock market for everyone, members of Congress and other government officials should be prohibited from using nonpublic information for financial benefit. The only special interest that will ever guide President Trump is the best interest of the American people.''\par \par \uc2 There is no blanket prohibition on government employees' placing bets on prediction markets, though prosecutors can still file fraud charges, as they did against Sgt. Van Dyke. He was also charged with unlawful use of confidential government information.\par \par \uc2 The White House issued a warning to staff last month against insider trading, amid a surge of suspicious trading on prediction markets, oil futures and stocks hinging on crucial moments in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 And the president's remarks on Thursday served as another warning.\par \par \uc2 But his own family's business interests could undermine the effectiveness of that message.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the publicly traded company that operates his social media platform, Truth Social, unveiled a new product called Truth Predict. The company, Trump Media & Technology Group, said the product would allow Truth Social users to trade prediction contracts ''related to major events and milestones, such as political elections, interest and inflation rate changes.''\par \par \uc2 Truth Predict is not yet operational, according to a company spokeswoman, who said that the product was being developed and tested before launch.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Donald Trump Jr., who is a Trump Media board member, said he was not involved in the decision to create Truth Predict. The spokesman, Andrew Surabian, said that it was not a board-level resolution and that Donald Trump Jr. was not a part of the company's day-to-day operations.\par \par \uc2 The younger Mr. Trump, however, has ties to both of the biggest prediction markets, Polymarket and its rival, Kalshi.\par \par \uc2 He is a paid adviser to Kalshi. And 1789 Capital, a venture capital firm in which Donald Trump Jr. is a partner, has invested in Polymarket. Mr. Trump is also an unpaid adviser to the company.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Surabian said that Donald Trump Jr.'s only involvement with prediction markets was to advise Kalshi and Polymarket on marketing strategies and to back Polymarket financially. He said that the president's son did not trade on their platforms and that he never interacted with the federal government on behalf of any company he had invested in or advised.\par \par \uc2 There is no indication that Donald Trump Jr. will now seek to influence his father's position on prediction markets. And it is unclear how firm the president's position is.\par \par \uc2 Even his complaint that the world was becoming ''somewhat of a casino'' was an odd remark, coming from him. Mr. Trump spent years owning casinos, though they all eventually closed or changed hands.\par \par \uc2 Megan Mineiro contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Megan Mineiro contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260425em4p00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
