\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Tube Strike In London Disrupts Some Lines\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Castle \par \uc2 678 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The dispute over working conditions and hours was expected to cause widespread delays and service outages.\par \par \uc2 London suffered widespread disruptions to its public transit system on Tuesday, as train drivers began a strike that affected vital parts of the British capital's underground rail network.\par \par \uc2 Transport for London\uc2 , the city transportation authority, said service on the \uc2 London Underground\uc2 , known as the Tube, had halted completely on one line, with several others severely disrupted. Trains that do run will be less frequent and very busy, and commuters should expect delays this week, the authority warned.\par \par \uc2 Train drivers are striking as part of a long-running dispute over their shift patterns, working conditions and terms of employment. Rising prices are squeezing living standards for many people in Britain, and other sectors have been hit by labor unrest, with many hospital doctors recently taking part in strikes.\par \par \uc2 Disruption to Tube services could have a significant economic impact. With more than three million journeys made each day on the network, stores, restaurants and businesses face potential financial losses if workers who can work from home avoid the capital.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody wants to see strike action or disruption for Londoners,'' the office of London's mayor, \uc2 Sadiq Khan\uc2 , said in a statement. ''Strikes have a serious impact on London's businesses and commuters.'' The mayor's office urged both sides to ''get around the table to resolve this matter and call off this planned strike action.''\par \par \uc2 How much of London's travel network is closing and when?\par \par \uc2 The strike action began at noon on Tuesday. On Wednesday evening and Thursday morning, services are expected to operate normally before the strike resumes again late Thursday morning. It is scheduled to end at noon on Friday, although disruptions are likely to continue into the early evening.\par \par \uc2 During the strike, no trains are expected to run at all on the Circle line, and the Metropolitan line was partly suspended on Tuesday. There were severe delays on several other routes.\par \par \uc2 Other transportation services and London's trams are expected to function normally as are a vast majority of the city's buses.\par \par \uc2 Why are train drivers striking?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Union of Rail, Maritime and Transport Workers\uc2 , which represents many drivers, wants a 32-hour week over five days. The transportation authority proposes reducing the working week for drivers from 36 hours to 35 hours over four days.\par \par \uc2 The union, also known as the R.M.T., says the authority's proposal amounts to five days' work compressed into four, prompting concern that drivers will be working longer, more tiring shifts, and that the resulting fatigue could affect safety.\par \par \uc2 The union also argues that the health and the life expectancy of London's Tube drivers are affected by shift patterns, exposure to dust particles and the psychological effect of suicides in the underground system.\par \par \uc2 Eddie Dempsey, the union's general secretary, accused \uc2 Transport for London\uc2  of seeming ''unwilling to make any concessions in a bid to avert strike action,'' adding that the approach would ''infuriate our members who want to see a negotiated settlement to this avoidable dispute.''\par \par \uc2 There are around 3,300 train drivers on the London Tube, and their annual salary is \u163\'00\'A371,160, or about $96,000, according to information provided by the authority in January. Another union representing train drivers, called \uc2 ASLEF\uc2 , accepted a contract proposal and was not striking.\par \par \uc2 What does London's transportation authority say?\par \par \uc2 Transport for London\uc2  argues that it is offering train drivers an additional day off while bringing the \uc2 London Underground\uc2  system in line with the working patterns of other British train companies, improving reliability and flexibility at no additional cost.\par \par \uc2 ''The changes would be voluntary,'' and those who wish to continue a five-day working week pattern would be able to do so, said Claire Mann, \uc2 Transport for London\uc2 's chief operating officer. She added, ''We urge the R.M.T. to call off this action, which will disrupt Londoners, and continue to engage with us.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260422em4m0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
