\par\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 Drop 'Retirement' From These Communities\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dina Cheney \par \uc2 1,378 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Chip Conley, 65, only watched a few episodes of the sitcom, ''The Golden Girls.'' But Mr. Conley, who co-founded Modern Elder Academy (M.E.A.), which offers retreats and courses that encourage embracing midlife and beyond, never forgot the lifestyle it depicted: four 50-plus women living together in a Miami home, for better or for worse.\par \par \uc2 Sure, the women had their squabbles, but they supported each other. ''What I appreciated about it was the independent living,'' said Mr. Conley, the author of ''Learning to Love Midlife,'' ''Wisdom at Work,'' and others. ''It showed women as having agency with camaraderie and a certain sauciness.''\par \par \uc2 Now Mr. Conley wants to make the kind of companionable co-living depicted on that show mainstream, with shared rental homes for his school's acolytes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Conley started the Modern Elder Academy with Jeff Hamaoui and Christine Sperber in 2018, with a mission ''to reframe aging'' by envisioning it as a time for personal transformation. Today, it offers days-long workshops on topics such as ''Cultivating Purpose'' and ''Navigating Transitions'' online and during stays at its two spalike campuses in El Pescadero, Mexico, and Santa Fe, N.M.\par \par \uc2 M.E.A.'s definition of ''elder'' is expansive. Generally, the founders consider it to be the second half of life, or the 50s, 70s and beyond. Since M.E.A.'s founding, more than 7,500 people have taken its seminars.\par \par \uc2 It was Mr. Hamaoui, 54, who proposed that they expand M.E.A. with a place for its followers to live in their later years. ''Jeff loved the idea of an intentional community that did not feel like a commune or kibbutz,'' said Mr. Conley, who before starting M.E.A. had founded the boutique hotel brand, Joie de Vivre Hospitality (now part of \uc2 Hyatt Hotels & Resorts\uc2 ) and later served as \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 's head of global hospitality and strategy. Situated on a two-acre farm, the community would be committed to values like sustainability and personal growth.\par \par \uc2 The result was Baja Sage, 26 townhouses owned by mostly female M.E.A. alumni. (As Mr. Conley explained, the population reflects the membership of the M.E.A. alumni community, 65 percent of which is female.) Mr. Conley and Mr. Hamaoui financed the project. The homes, which cost between $300,000 and $500,000, were initially offered for sale in 2019 right before the pandemic, and were move-in ready about one year ago.\par \par \uc2 After the Baja Sage homes sold quickly, Mr. Conley conducted surveys and focus groups to assess M.E.A. alumni interest in co-living. Eighty-eight percent of respondents said they'd consider living with friends, and more than half noted they'd prefer it to aging alone or in a conventional retirement community.\par \par \uc2 Encouraged by these results, Mr. Conley decided to create shared rental homes near the Santa Fe M.E.A. campus. To avoid the hassles and inefficiencies of new construction, he bought existing large houses to convert into shared homes with small furnished private units and common areas.\par \par \uc2 Currently, there is one three-bedroom home for three residents and one six-bedroom that will most likely house five women. Within the next couple of years, Mr. Conley plans to repeat the process with several more homes. He also plans to offer a co-owned version of this style of housing.\par \par \uc2 Monthly rents, which will include access to campus amenities like a gym and hiking trails, will range from $1,950 to $3,750 and decrease by 1 percent annually after two years. ''We want to create stability,'' said Mr. Conley. ''As people get older, they're on more of a fixed income. You want people to feel safe and loved.''\par \par \uc2 Typically, roommate searches are associated with college students or 20-somethings. But M.E.A. will help match potential housemates and possibly organize residences around specific interests or values, like pet-friendly or multigenerational housing. ''There is something deep about having community that is values- and purpose-aligned,'' said Mr. Hamaoui. ''A community can thrive when it has a purpose that is larger than itself.''\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth White, 71, a Washington, D.C.-based author and age-solutions activist, is considering securing one of the rental units.\par \par \uc2 While attending a workshop at M.E.A. 10 years ago, Ms. White realized she wanted her future home to be similar in feel to the campus, which she described as ''beautiful and lush'' with ''an atmosphere that supported mindfulness and well-being.''\par \par \uc2 ''It was my first time really thinking in a structured way about what my future could be, thinking that I had more agency than I believed to shape the future I wanted,'' Ms. White said. ''Co-living in a community of like-minded people would be energizing and grounding.''\par \par \uc2 When it comes to housemate matching, Mr. Conley said, ''our first priority is to find women who already know each other and want to get a home together (college friends, work friends and M.E.A. alums who've become friends).'' Potential tenants will also have the opportunity to self-select housemates at M.E.A. co-living workshops.\par \par \uc2 M.E.A. held the first of these in mid-January and is hosting additional ones in April and May. Of the 19 women who attended, eight are planning to trial one of the two homes, which will be ready for move-in this spring, said Mr. Conley.\par \par \uc2 One of them is Robin Tefft, 71, a retired registered dietitian based in San Antonio, Texas. At the workshop, she met another participant with whom she is considering sharing a one-year lease. Each would live there half the year, so they could test out the experience of sharing a home with other women.\par \par \uc2 Fellow attendees, Dana Collie, 57, and Karen Jordan Markham, 75, are also considering renting a unit this year as a trial. They too plan to share a lease to determine whether this type of housing arrangement will work for them long term. Ms. Collie, who works in nonprofit consulting, first became intrigued by a residential M.E.A. experience when she attended a workshop on the Baja campus in 2021.\par \par \uc2 ''I remember thinking very clearly, 'Yes, this would be a very cool way to live.'''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Collie said she has made several friends through M.E.A. and is looking for ''housemates who align around common values and expectations, where expenses, daily responsibilities, and companionship are shared in ways that feel supportive.''\par \par \uc2 For the past several years, Ms. Markham, an executive coach in Austin, Texas, has been investigating cities where she might spend ''the last quarter'' of her life, she said. ''Without the right community you have nothing, especially as you age.'' Ms. Markham is optimistic she might have found the right one. Since the workshop, she has been in touch with all the other attendees through their WhatsApp group, which she described as ''very active.''\par \par \uc2 The homes Mr. Conley is creating are intended for the M.E.A. community (although alumni status is not a requirement). Yet Mr. Conley is hopeful they'll inspire a larger movement around companionable co-living. With life spans increasing, ''the idea of retiring at 65 and living 25 years playing bingo and drinking martinis doesn't appeal to a lot of people,'' said Mr. Conley, who said he prefers the phrase ''regenerative communities'' to ''retirement communities.''\par \par \uc2 Th\u233\'00\'E9r\u232\'00\'E8se O'Neill, 56, who relocated from the San Francisco Bay Area to Baja Sage in 2024 and now serves as lead facilitator on the Mexico campus, shares his optimism. ''Every week I teach, I hear from people who are lonely in their 50s and 60s,'' she said. ''Perhaps they retired and all their friends were work friends. Perhaps they are single and afraid of aging alone. Whatever the reason, it seems people now more than ever are longing for belonging. M.E.A. and Baja Sage provide that in powerful ways.''\par \par \uc2 She recalled a dance party celebrating a resident's birthday. ''The vision of all these friends and neighbors dancing, uninhibited, under the cafe lights and Baja stars was so joyful,'' she said. ''It's truly why I wanted to live here.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260419em4j00072\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
