\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As War Chills Economy, Dubai's Most Vulnerable Bear the Cost\b0\par\par\uc2 By Raja Abdulrahim \par \uc2 1,346 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Migrant workers in the United Arab Emirates, whose hospitality industry has been hit hard by the war with Iran, are facing furloughs, pay cuts and even repatriation.\par \par \uc2 When the war with Iran began in late February, customers hunting for bargains on designer wares stopped coming to the Outlet Mall Dubai and a saleswoman named Marjorie was forced to go on leave for a month.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks later, she said, her furlough was extended until June 1 without pay.\par \par \uc2 ''All we do is worry about this situation and think 'What if they repatriate us?''' Marjorie, a 32-year-old migrant worker from the Philippines, said in a recent interview. ''If this continues for three months, maybe we will go back.''\par \par \uc2 The United Arab Emirates and its major metropolis, Dubai, have been spared much of the destruction and casualties that other countries in the region have suffered in the war. That is thanks largely to the interception of most of the drones and missiles that Iran has fired at it -- more than 2,500 -- in retaliation for the assault launched by the United States and Israel.\par \par \uc2 The war has nevertheless taken a toll on the economy in Dubai, one of the major financial powerhouses in the region, and especially on the tourism and hospitality industries. Many expatriate residents have fled the country and tourists have largely stayed away.\par \par \uc2 Now, five-star hotels sit mostly empty and big events such as an art fair and book fair have been postponed. On a recent Sunday night, the \uc2 Dubai International Airport\uc2 , one of the world's busiest, was nearly empty. All restaurants and coffee shops in the terminal were closed.\par \par \uc2 Dubai's most vulnerable workers, like Marjorie, who power lucrative industries such as construction, hospitality and tourism, have lost jobs, been furloughed, had their wages cut and even been temporarily sent back to their home countries without pay.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  reported that migrant workers in Gulf Arab states like the Emirates have been unduly affected by the war, facing risks to both their lives and their job security. The rights group called on employers to uphold contractual obligations despite the conflict.\par \par \uc2 The Emirates has some 8.7 million migrant workers, more than 80 percent of the population. That makes it one of the world's biggest hosts of a foreign labor force, according to the \uc2 International Labor Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The war paused last week when the United States and Iran agreed to a two-week cease-fire. But tensions escalated again after Iranian and American negotiators met over the weekend without reaching agreement. President Trump then announced a naval blockade of Iranian ports.\par \par \uc2 The Emirati government has not commented on the war's impact on its economy, but it has announced two relief packages for the tourism industry. Before the conflict began, the government had said it was focusing on developing the tourism sector as a central driver of economic growth.\par \par \uc2 In January, not long before the U.S.-Israeli attack on Iran began, the tourism and economy minister, Abdulla bin Touq al-Marri, pointed to the growth of the tourism industry. In 2025, he said, the sector generated more than $79 billion -- equivalent to about 15 percent of national gross domestic product. Four years earlier, it was just 6 percent, he said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the importance of the industries they prop up, migrant workers in the Emirates and across the Middle East often live paycheck to paycheck, sending money home to support their families. Now the war has made that precarious financial situation even worse.\par \par \uc2 In its report, \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  said that businesses should protect workers during economic slumps rather than shift the burden entirely to them. Many of the migrants have families back home to feed, as well as job recruitment loans to repay, it noted.\par \par \uc2 ''The conflict has brought new risks to migrant workers while also exposing the gaps in labor and other rights,'' said \uc2 Michael Page\uc2 , deputy Middle East and North Africa director at \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many Gulf countries rely heavily on foreign labor and have long been criticized for the exploitation and abuse of some migrant workers. Some of the workers live in overcrowded and squalid labor camps, have had their passports confiscated by employers and face harsh reprisals if they dare to speak out.\par \par \uc2 The Emirati government did not respond to questions about how foreign workers have been affected by the war or what protections were in place for them.\par \par \uc2 ''Tourism is more or less at a standstill at the moment,'' said Mandy Kouwenberg, founder and managing director of Limitless, a hospitality recruitment agency in Dubai specializing in five-star hotels and high-end restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Businesses hit by the downturn have come up with different ways of dealing with their staff, she said. Some hotels have reached out to ask if she has any hospitality job openings their furloughed workers can fill.\par \par \uc2 ''Are redundancies taking place? Yes,'' she said. ''And some companies are reducing salaries, because their expenses are still going and they need to reduce their costs as much as they can.''\par \par \uc2 Many of the hotels Ms. Kouwenberg works with provide accommodations and meals to their employees, so even those without a job at the moment have decided to stay, she said.\par \par \uc2 Migrant workers in the Emirates, like those in other Gulf countries, were already paying a high price in this war. All 10 of the civilians who have been killed in the country were foreign workers, according to the Ministry of Defense. Most were killed by debris from intercepted Iranian missiles or drones.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie, who asked to be identified by her first name only for fear of retribution by her employer, said that for now, she was doing manicures and pedicures for roommates and friends, making a few dollars here and there.\par \par \uc2 Her husband, who works in a restaurant, still has his job. But with the money they need to send home to their family, including their 1-year-old child, the couple have had to cut back to eating just one meal a day, she said.\par \par \uc2 Muhammad Faisal, a 45-year-old construction worker from Sri Lanka, has been placed on leave from his job since the start of the war. Though he is still receiving his salary, there are no more opportunities for overtime, which he relied on to supplement his salary of 1,600 dirham a month, about $435. Most of that, he said, he sends home to Sri Lanka to support his wife, two children and his in-laws.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Faisal doesn't know how long he will be on leave, especially because the import of construction materials has been hampered by the conflict.\par \par \uc2 He plans to eat mostly lentils and rice -- no meat -- so he can keep sending remittances home. ''I don't care if I don't eat,'' he said. ''As long as I can help them.''\par \par \uc2 Even those still working are wracked with uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 ''We are still waiting for our salary,'' said Lynette Macepel, 34, whose pay from her supermarket job was two days late when she spoke to The New York Times.\par \par \uc2 Many of her friends who work in restaurants have had their salaries slashed even though they are working the same hours, she said as she waited with a friend at a bus stop. Others have lost their jobs entirely.\par \par \uc2 ''They are thinking how to pay rent, food, transport, anything like this,'' Ms. Macepel said. ''So some of our roommates go for repatriation.''\par \par \uc2 She herself was waiting for her wages so she could send money back home to the Philippines to support three daughters, ages 8, 9 and 12.\par \par \uc2 She was also dealing with another source of anxiety: Her work visa is nearing expiration and her employer had yet to tell her whether it would be renewed, ''maybe because of this war,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 She hopes to keep working in Dubai.\par \par \uc2 ''I need money,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Anjana Sankar contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Anjana Sankar contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260415em4f0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
