\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Long Island Train Riders Brace for a Strike in May\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefanos Chen \par \uc2 1,152 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers could shut down America's busiest passenger rail service beginning on May 16 if they do not reach a deal with transit officials.\par \par \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  workers are prepared to go on strike as early as May 16 if they can't agree on a new contract with transit officials, labor leaders said on Wednesday -- a move that would be a major blow to America's busiest passenger rail service.\par \par \uc2 ''We're talking about a complete and total shutdown,'' said Kevin Sexton, a vice president of the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , one of the unions ready to strike. He added, ''The clock is ticking.''\par \par \uc2 Five unions representing more than 3,500 workers have threatened for months to walk off the job unless they receive bigger raises than other divisions of the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , the state agency that runs the railroad.\par \par \uc2 The unions, which represent engineers, machinists, signalmen and other jobs critical to the rail operation, are seeking a retroactive 9.5 percent wage increase covering the last three years -- the same offered to many other New York transit and civil servant unions. But they also want an additional 5 percent raise starting in 2026.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. has argued that such a divergence in pay would upset the typical pattern for wage increases established with other groups, and would not be feasible unless the unions compromised on other aspects of the contract.\par \par \uc2 The unions had asked for two federal boards to review the case, but their recommendations did not lead to the resolution of the dispute. The unions were granted the right to strike as early as 12:01 a.m. on Saturday, May 16.\par \par \uc2 The federal government could still prevent a work stoppage, but Congress did not intervene in a similar dispute last year, when \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  workers went on strike.\par \par \uc2 A strike on the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , which carries more than 270,000 passengers a day between Long Island and New York City, would be a nightmare for commuters, many of whom have few other ways to get to work. And the disruptions would take place in the middle of an election year for Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat, who is set to face Bruce Blakeman, a Republican with close ties to Long Island as the Nassau County executive.\par \par \uc2 The strike would be the first to take place on the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  since 1994, when a two-day suspension sent riders scrambling for alternatives.\par \par \uc2 The railroad's work force is already among the highest paid in the nation, with cash compensation for members of the five holdout unions averaging over $136,000 in 2024, according to M.T.A. figures. The holdout unions have not received a raise since 2022, and said they were seeking only to keep up with the high cost of living in the New York City area, one of the most expensive places in the country.\par \par \uc2 Janno Lieber\uc2 , the chief executive and chair of the M.T.A., called the idea of a strike ''insane'' at a board meeting last month. He said he was open to negotiating about salaries, but only if the union was willing to give up some rules that could greatly increase the costs of certain kinds of work.\par \par \uc2 For instance, if an engineer drives a diesel train at the start of a shift, but is asked to switch to an electric train in the same day, the M.T.A. is required to compensate that worker with two days' pay. And if, on the same day, the engineer is asked to switch from driving passengers to driving a train back to a yard for maintenance or storage, that worker is entitled to a third day of pay.\par \par \uc2 These so-called penalty payments added almost 15 percent to the average engineer's compensation in 2024, the M.T.A. said.\par \par \uc2 But the holdout unions, which represent about half of the railroad's work force, were emboldened by the support of two federally appointed boards that were called to weigh in on the negotiations. Both boards argued that the unions should be paid more in 2026 than what the M.T.A. had offered. Union leaders said they were unwilling to make concessions on work rules in the new contract.\par \par \uc2 The recommendations are not binding, and Mr. Lieber has suggested that the boards, whose members were appointed by President Trump, were politically motivated to side with the unions.\par \par \uc2 John McCarthy, the chief of policy and external relations at the M.T.A., said both sides were in agreement on three years of pay raises, and that the additional demands could still be resolved in negotiations.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody wins in a strike -- not the unions, not the L.I.R.R. and not the Long Islanders who depend on our great service,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul's office declined to comment, deferring instead to the authority's statement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blakeman said in a statement that, as county executive, he had helped negotiate labor contracts and would do the same as governor.\par \par \uc2 Unlike several other divisions of the M.T.A., which are generally prevented from striking because they are governed by different rules, the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  is covered by a more permissive 1926 federal law called the Railway Labor Act.\par \par \uc2 Work stoppages are still very rare under the law, but in an unusual move, the federal agency that oversees these labor disputes, the National Mediation Board, released the five unions in August from ongoing mediation, clearing the path for a potential strike.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. has said it would run free bus service at a limited number of busy stations during a strike, especially for commuters who cannot work remotely, but that there was no alternative that could effectively handle the volume of riders who relied on the railroad.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Lieber, the M.T.A. chair, has shown no signs of retreat, arguing at a recent board meeting that a strike would be ''flushing money down the toilet,'' because workers would squander much of the salary increase they are seeking after three unpaid days on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 Even if the contract dispute is settled soon, the long delay in reaching a deal means that the unions will begin negotiating another contract, for 2027 and beyond, within the year.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Klein, the general chairman of the International Brotherhood of Electrical Workers, one of the holdout unions, said in an interview that the M.T.A. was underestimating their resolve.\par \par \uc2 ''To sit there and infer that somehow the organizations are just looking to blow off steam and this would just be a three-day stoppage, I think is a serious miscalculation,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 ''Our memberships are prepared to do what's necessary, if it comes to it.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260409em490004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
