\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 ProPublica Journalists Strike For a Day, Partly Over A.I.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Other sticking points for journalists at the investigative nonprofit included demands for wage increases and layoff protections.\par \par \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  journalists walked off the job on Wednesday, for 24 hours, after more than two years of negotiations that failed to yield a deal for a union contract.\par \par \uc2 It was the first major labor action at \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , one of the country's largest nonprofit newsrooms. About 150 of its workers, including reporters, copy editors and communications staff, unionized in 2023 and have been negotiating a contract since.\par \par \uc2 The core disagreements were over wage increases, layoff protections and the use of artificial intelligence, said Katie Campbell, a video journalist who has worked at \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  for eight years. She said workers wanted a say in how A.I. was being adopted at the company, protections against being replaced by the technology and transparency for readers about its use.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 ProPublica\uc2  is its workers, and when more than 100 people step away from their work for a day, that's significant,'' Ms. Campbell said, adding, ''We want to get back to work, but we want a fair contract to do this work.''\par \par \uc2 Newsrooms around the country are grappling with similar issues over the use of A.I. in reporting, editing and disseminating journalism. Some embarrassing errors, such as \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 's corrections for A.I.-generated summaries or the inadvertent publication of A.I.-written articles, have raised the stakes. Collective bargaining agreements at unionized newsrooms increasingly contain language that offer protections against replacing jobs with A.I. and guardrails for its use.\par \par \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 's union is represented by the NewsGuild of New York, which also represents some journalists at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  spokeswoman said the company had made proposals that mirrored those accepted by the NewsGuild at other news organizations. She said \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  had never had layoffs.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 ProPublica\uc2  is committed to reaching a fair and sustainable first contract to cement the strong pay and benefits we've always provided our staff,'' the spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 The unionized workers' authorization for the strike took place on March 20, with 92 percent of members voting in favor. On Monday, the union filed an unfair-labor-practice charge with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  against \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , accusing it of failing to bargain with the union before putting in place an A.I. policy.\par \par \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 's policy says any work done with the assistance of A.I. will be reviewed by journalists and disclosed to readers.\par \par \uc2 Tyson Evans, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 's chief product and brand officer, said the company had shown the principles to the bargaining committee before publishing them.\par \par \uc2 The union has asked supporters not to visit the \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  website or read its content during Wednesday's strike.\par \par \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , which was founded nearly 20 years ago, relies on support from philanthropic foundations and individual donors. Its investigative reporting has been awarded multiple Pulitzer Prizes.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260409em490003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
