\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 U.S. Reports Strong Job Growth For March, Beating Predictions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lydia DePillis \par \uc2 1,303 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Payrolls expanded and unemployment dropped last month after a health care strike ended and a harsh winter abated.\par \par \uc2 The labor market put in a strong showing in March, as wintry weather receded, strikes concluded and businesses started looking beyond the huge changes of President Trump's first year in office.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department reported on Friday that employers had added 178,000 jobs last month, substantially more than economists had expected, bringing the average over the past three volatile months to a healthy 68,000 positions. The unemployment rate dropped to 4.3 percent, leveling off after a gradual rise, as the work force contracted slightly.\par \par \uc2 The numbers were collected before the energy price shock caused by the U.S.-Israeli war with Iran tightened its grip on the global economy, however. Forecasters have estimated that persistently higher oil prices will slow job creation and raise unemployment in a year when they had expected the economy to regain some vigor.\par \par \uc2 ''A lot of people thought this was going to be the year that finally we put political uncertainty behind us and the labor market was going to reaccelerate,'' said Guy Berger, chief economist at \uc2 Homebase\uc2 , a scheduling and payroll platform. ''And it's still too early for gas prices to meaningfully affect the labor market, so maybe this is a 'what might have been.'''\par \par \uc2 The job surge in March will most likely give the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  some comfort as the central bank tries to balance stubborn inflation with a soft labor market. This is the last employment report before its next meeting at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 As has been the case for the past few years, gains were led by health care and social assistance professions, which packed on 90,000 jobs. That number includes 31,000 workers who ended a strike at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  in California, but even without them, the increase was slightly above the rate at which the sectors have been growing over the past year.\par \par \uc2 The easing of a stormy, icy winter also helped to boost leisure and hospitality employers, which added 44,000 workers. The changing weather may also have propelled construction, which grew by 26,000 jobs. That industry has been surging since last fall, primarily because of the industrial jobs created by the data center boom.\par \par \uc2 It's not clear that this pace can continue for much longer. The share of the population that is employed has been dropping and now stands at 59.2 percent, driven both by aging demographics and the Trump administration's harsh immigration restrictions. As the available work force contracts, the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas\uc2  estimated this week that the break-even rate of jobs needed to absorb new workers could be negative.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a whole new world,'' said Laura Ullrich, director of economic research at the job search website Indeed. ''The idea that all of a sudden, a report of 50,000 jobs could be a good report, that's hard to wrap your brain around. Now we're at zero to 30,000.''\par \par \uc2 There are other signs of declining dynamism. The hiring rate in February sunk to a low last reached in 2020, when the economy was largely shut down. Surveys by the \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2  have shown that the share of business owners with jobs they can't fill has been falling, and only 12 percent plan to add jobs in the next three months.\par \par \uc2 Consistent with that frozen picture, average hourly earnings grew only 3.5 percent in March from a year ago, the slowest pace since 2021 and only slightly faster than inflation. That may also be a consequence of shifting composition, as more jobs are added in relatively low-wage social assistance roles and subtracted from higher-paid sectors like financial services and computer systems design.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, layoffs have remained extremely low, as companies have sought to retain talent rather than let people go only to rehire them later. The average workweek shortened to 34.2 hours, indicating that employers are cutting hours rather than head count. The federal government is a notable exception, having shed a total of 355,000 positions, or 11.8 percent, since reaching a peak in October 2024.\par \par \uc2 ''You may not be hiring aggressively or you may even be downsizing through attrition, but at this point there's no clear impetus to aggressively downsize,'' said \uc2 Brett Ryan\uc2 , an economist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jacqueline Bonanno has noticed the balance of power shifting in favor of employers, as hiring went from being a challenge before the pandemic to ''very easy'' today. Ms. Bonanno, who has about 300 employees across six restaurants in Denver, said that both recruiting and retention improved after she instituted a service charge that raised her staff's take-home pay.\par \par \uc2 But the business has also benefited from rough times across the rest of the restaurant industry, where staffing only returned to prepandemic levels in October. The last time Ms. Bonanno posted a job opening at her downtown pizzeria, she got 350 applications, including from people switching from other fields where opportunities have diminished.\par \par \uc2 ''With the economy the way it is, we're in a unique position to get the cream of the crop,'' Ms. Bonanno said. ''Because restaurants have failed, and we end up with really talented people who've lost their jobs.''\par \par \uc2 From a worker's perspective, this labor market looks fairly secure for those who have jobs, but with few opportunities for those trying to find new positions. The typical person switching jobs now sees wage growth of 4.4 percent, down from 7.7 percent three years ago, according to the Atlanta Fed. The number of workers who have been unemployed for more than 27 weeks has remained unusually high for an economy that is not in a recession.\par \par \uc2 Fabrice Ulysse was one of the few who managed to get a new job last year. After cutting short a mathematics doctorate in 2023 when variable-rate student loans from his undergraduate degree became too much to bear, he took a position in I.T. at a hospital in New York and taught as an adjunct professor on the side. After a long search for something closer to his field, he landed a job as a math teacher at the private high school on the Upper East Side.\par \par \uc2 To get both positions, Mr. Ulysse said, he benefited from connections -- his mother worked at the hospital, and he graduated from the school where he now teaches.\par \par \uc2 ''When I was looking for a job last year, I literally didn't get an interview unless it was through someone that I knew,'' Mr. Ulysse said. ''I'm very lucky they could pull those strings for me. That's not a given.''\par \par \uc2 The March report is a snapshot of the economy before the impact of the war in the Middle East began to be felt in earnest. The resulting spike in gasoline, diesel and fertilizer prices is expected to force consumers to cut back spending on other priorities that employ people, like going out to eat and taking long road trips. Goldman Sachs estimates that the oil price shock will reduce monthly employment growth by 10,000 jobs through 2026.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, trade uncertainty, persistently high interest rates and immigration enforcement activities have prompted economists to mark up their expectations of a recession next year.\par \par \uc2 ''The labor market has been really resilient, but I don't know if we've seen the combination of everything that's happening right now,'' said Elizabeth Crofoot, an economist at the employment data firm Lightcast. ''And I'm not sure how much further we can hold on.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260404em440003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
